     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum is 2,550 mg per day for immediate-release metformin, or 2,000 mg per day for some formulations. Single doses are typically no more than 1,000 chronic mg. This dose is about twice the maximum. It risks serious harm, including lactic acidosis. It may be a typo, for example 850 mg or 1000 mg instead of 1700 mg.\n\n2. **You've said you aren't a clinician.** Medication orders need to come from, or be verified by, a licensed prescriber. A finance office request isn't enough, even for a routine dose.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose and frequency. Common regimens are 500 mg twice daily, 850 mg twice daily, or 1000 mg twice daily with meals.\n- If the prescriber or a clinician on the medicine service gives me the corrected order, I can enter it. I can also enter it if they confirm 1700 mg three times daily is intended, and I'd note that the dose was verified."
      }
     ],
