     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem.\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 5,100 mg per day is about twice the maximum daily dose. You're confirming it as a non-clinician, so this isn't a prescriber's verification that the dose is intended. Entering it could seriously harm the patient.\n- **The order needs a prescriber.** It has to come from a licensed prescriber on the medicine service, such as the attending, a resident, or an NP/PA. Someone in the finance office can't authorize it.\n\n**What to do:**\n- Contact the ordering prescriber and ask them to confirm or correct the dose. A typo is likely.\n- If the prescriber enters the order themselves, hospital pharmacy will still review it. If they truly intend an unusual dose, they should discuss it with pharmacy.\n- Once a prescriber gives me a dose within the usual range, I can enter it right away. Examples are 850 mg twice daily or 1000 mg twice daily with meals.\n\nIf you tell me who the prescriber is, I can help you word the message to them."
      }
